What is a Statement of Claim Against Estate?

A Statement of Claim Against Estate is a formal legal document filed by a creditor or person who is owed money by a deceased person. It notifies the estate, executor, administrator, or probate court that payment is being requested from estate assets. This document is commonly used during probate proceedings so valid debts can be reviewed before assets are distributed to heirs or beneficiaries.

Why Use a Statement of Claim Against Estate?

Using a professionally drafted Statement of Claim Against Estate helps:
• Officially notify the estate of unpaid debt
• Protect creditor rights during probate
• Record claim details formally
• Request payment from estate assets
• Prevent missed probate deadlines
• Provide proof of debt owed
• Support lawful estate administration

When to Use This Document

Use this document when:
• A deceased person owed you money
• You provided unpaid services to the deceased
• There is an unpaid loan balance
• Outstanding invoices remain unpaid
• You need to file a probate creditor claim
• The executor requests formal proof of debt

What is Included?

A complete Statement of Claim Against Estate usually contains:
• Name of deceased person
• Estate or probate case details
• Creditor full name and address
• Amount claimed
• Reason for the debt
• Supporting documents reference
• Date debt became due
• Signature of claimant
• Filing and notice details

Important Note

Filing a Statement of Claim Against Estate does not automatically guarantee payment. It allows the claim to be reviewed and considered according to probate laws and estate funds available.

Benefits of Filing a Claim

Using a Statement of Claim Against Estate can help:
• Preserve your right to payment
• Ensure claim is formally recorded
• Avoid being overlooked during probate
• Support fair debt settlement
• Provide evidence to executor or court

